---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { commands, categories } from '../data/commands';
---

<DocsLayout title="Skills Reference" sectionLabel="How It Works" activeSlug="skills">

  <div class="panel-intro">
    <h2>Skills Reference</h2>
    <p>Every slash command maps to a <strong>skill</strong> &mdash; a <code>SKILL.md</code> file in the <code>skills/</code> directory. Skills range from zero-cost script-only operations (like <code>/idea</code> and <code>/status</code>) to complex multi-agent orchestrations (like <code>/new-project</code> and <code>/wrap</code>). Use the category filters to browse all available skills.</p>
    <div class="key-points">
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>What they are</strong>
          <p><code>SKILL.md</code> files with YAML frontmatter (name, description, arguments) and a markdown body defining the full workflow: preconditions, steps, error handling, and agent routing. Some skills are script-only (zero tokens), others spin up single or multiple agents.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>What problem they solve</strong>
          <p>Skills turn complex multi-step workflows into single commands. Instead of writing a detailed prompt for "run tests, calculate score, generate docs, commit, and log session," you type <code>/wrap</code> and the skill definition handles every step, error path, and agent handoff.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Modern Skill Patterns ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Modern Skill Patterns</h3>
    <p>VibeCrew skills leverage advanced Claude Code features for better autocomplete, cost optimization, tool scoping, and native subagent isolation.</p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Frontmatter Fields</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>argument-hint</code></td><td>Shows placeholder text in autocomplete after the skill name</td><td><code>"User Authentication"</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>allowed-tools</code></td><td>Restricts which tools the skill can use &mdash; enforces read-only for dashboards</td><td><code>Read, Bash, Glob, Grep</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>Routes skill to a specific model for cost optimization</td><td><code>haiku</code> for dashboards</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>context: fork</code></td><td>Runs the skill as an isolated subagent &mdash; results return to main session</td><td>Used by <code>/review</code>, <code>/audit</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>agent:</code></td><td>Specifies which agent runs in a forked context</td><td><code>code-reviewer</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>category</code></td><td>Classifies skill for evaluation and retirement tracking</td><td><code>workflow</code>, <code>analysis</code>, <code>action</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Dynamic Injection (<code>!</code> Backtick)</h4>
    <p>Skills can pre-load data at load time using <code>!</code> backtick syntax. The shell command runs before Claude sees the prompt, eliminating the first &ldquo;read state&rdquo; turn and saving tokens.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>What Gets Injected</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>/status</code></td><td>state.json, backlog.json, git branch, recent commits, latest score</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/cost</code></td><td>Session cost data, aggregated spending</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/check</code></td><td>package.json (scripts section)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/wrap</code></td><td>state.json, backlog.json, git diff --stat</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/fix-issue</code></td><td>Git provider auth check</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/heal</code></td><td>CI logs, provider auth check</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Native Argument Substitution</h4>
    <p>Skills use <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> and <code>$0</code> for native argument parsing instead of manual extraction steps. <code>$0</code> captures the first argument, <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> captures the full argument string.</p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Forked Skills</h4>
    <p>Four analysis skills use <code>context: fork</code> with a dedicated <code>agent:</code> to run in native subagent isolation. The forked agent produces formatted output directly and returns results to the main session.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>Agent</th><th>Why Forked</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>/review</code></td><td>Code Reviewer</td><td>Read-only analysis &mdash; clean single-agent scope</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/simplify</code></td><td>Code Simplifier</td><td>Analysis-only; <code>/apply-simplifications</code> handles interactive changes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/audit</code></td><td>Security Auditor</td><td>OWASP scan with pre-injected dependency + secret data</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/system-review</code></td><td>System Reviewer</td><td>Cross-project telemetry analysis</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Intent-Based Triggering</h4>
    <p>
      You don&apos;t need to memorize all 38 commands. VibeCrew includes an intent detection layer that matches
      natural language to the right skill. For example, saying &ldquo;fix a bug&rdquo; suggests <code>/fix-issue</code>,
      and &ldquo;check quality&rdquo; suggests <code>/check</code>. Intent detection runs automatically during
      session startup and when the Orchestrator processes your requests.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full mapping is defined in <code>templates/intent-map.md</code> and covers all core workflows:
      project setup, planning, development, quality, session management, CI/CD, analysis, and gamification.
      Slash commands remain the primary interface &mdash; intent detection is a convenience layer for discoverability.
    </p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Skill Categories</h4>
    <p>Every skill has a <code>category</code> field that determines its evaluation strategy and retirement criteria.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Count</th><th>Evaluation Strategy</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>workflow</strong></td><td>7 skills</td><td>Durable (preference-encoding) &mdash; optimize triggers, rarely retire</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>analysis</strong></td><td>6 skills</td><td>Capability-uplift &mdash; A/B test after model upgrades</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>action</strong></td><td>8 skills</td><td>Capability-uplift &mdash; A/B test after model upgrades</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>dashboard</strong></td><td>4 skills</td><td>Durable &mdash; read-only, optimize for speed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>utility</strong></td><td>10 skills</td><td>Durable &mdash; simple operations, minimal maintenance</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <div class="skills-filters" id="skills-filters">
    <button class="filter-pill active" data-cat="all">All</button>
    {categories.map((cat) => (
      <button class="filter-pill" data-cat={cat}>{cat}</button>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="skills-table-wrap">
    <table class="agent-table" id="skills-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Command</th>
          <th>Category</th>
          <th>Agent(s)</th>
          <th>Tier</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {commands.map((cmd) => (
          <tr data-cat={cmd.category}>
            <td><code>{cmd.name}{cmd.args ? ` ${cmd.args}` : ''}</code></td>
            <td><span class="cat-badge">{cmd.category}</span></td>
            <td>{cmd.agent}</td>
            <td>{cmd.tier}</td>
            <td>{cmd.purpose}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .skills-filters {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .filter-pill {
    padding: 0.3rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 100px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: all 0.15s;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
  }

  .filter-pill:hover {
    color: var(--color-text);
    border-color: var(--color-accent-dim);
  }

  .filter-pill.active {
    background: var(--color-accent-bg);
    color: var(--color-accent);
    border-color: rgba(167, 139, 250, 0.2);
  }

  .skills-table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
</style>

<script is:inline>
  (function() {
    var pills = document.querySelectorAll('.filter-pill');
    var skillRows = document.querySelectorAll('#skills-table tbody tr');
    pills.forEach(function(pill) {
      pill.addEventListener('click', function() {
        pills.forEach(function(p) { p.classList.remove('active'); });
        pill.classList.add('active');
        var cat = pill.getAttribute('data-cat');
        skillRows.forEach(function(row) {
          var match = cat === 'all' || row.getAttribute('data-cat') === cat;
          row.style.display = match ? '' : 'none';
        });
      });
    });
  })();
</script>
